  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry conditions with above normal temperatures\npersist through the weekend. This will worsen ongoing water \nresource drought conditions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure will build across the region tonight through \nSaturday, then exit to the east Sunday. No precipitation is\nexpected through the weekend along with above normal level \ntemperatures.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain is likely Sunday night and Monday. Enough \nrain to make a small dent in the drought is likely.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nThe north/west (wetter) trend in the models/ensembles continues,\nstill showing soaking rain is likely for the whole area. That said,\nmodel agreement is still lower than usual for a system of this\nstrength at this time range. There is uncertainty in the speed,\nstrength, and path of the low pressure as it moves northeast up\nthe East Coast. That said, there is enough confidence to\ncontinue to raise rain chances for the whole area. \n\nThis will be a quick-hitting system, with pretty much all the\nrain falling within 12 hours. That said, rain totals could be\npretty impressive along and north/west of the low pressure\ncenter track. The low could track through central Maine, through\nthe middle of the Gulf of Maine, or anywhere in between. It is\nstill too early to tell. Rain totals are very uncertain\ndepending on the low track, but it is likely that somewhere in \nthe area will exceed an inch of rain. Keep in mind this is all\nwithin a 12 hour period, so it will be pretty heavy. While \nflash flooding is unlikely, any leaf-clogged drain could cause \nlocalized issues. The probabilities for the heavier rain still \ncontinue to be higher for Downeast with totals between \n1.25-1.75, and northern areas between .25-.75 of rain. \nProbability for 2 inches of rain is less then 20 percent area \nwide. Probabilities could increase if the low tracks closer to \nthe shore.\n\nThere is no risk of snow with this system, as the airmass will\nbe quite mild for this time of year. \n\nWinds along/north of the low pressure track appear pretty light,\nbut anywhere south of the low track could have some decent south\nwinds. If the low favors a track on the northern envelope of\nsolutions, some gusts to around 35 mph are possible along the\ncoast.\n\nRain ends by Monday evening, colder air rushes in behind it.\nStill believe that there will be enough of a gap between the \nend of the rain and temperatures falling below freezing that a \nwidespread flash freeze of roads is unlikely, but cannot rule \nout isolated icy spots Tuesday morning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...An active weather pattern is likely from \nThanksgiving into next weekend. Forecast confidence on any \nparticular weather system is very low, but travelers should \ncontinue to monitor this period for potential wintry weather.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\nWhile it is way too far in the future to pinpoint any \nparticular weather system, it is important to note that the \ngeneral weather pattern from Thanksgiving through the weekend \nafter Thanksgiving is favorable for active weather. This is \nespecially important given the active travel period. Looking \nthrough various ensemble model solutions, timing and system\nstrength are all over the place. That said, the pattern will be\nfavorable for Noreasters. Models are continuing to show a \nsignal for snow during the Thanksgiving weekend for Northern \nand Eastern Maine. We do not want people to be alarmed or alter \nand travel plans yet, but rather to be aware of the potential \nand continue to check back with us for updates.",
